{
 "dataset": "Cumulative CO2 by collective \u2014 the responsibility-allocation basis",
 "_source": "Our World in Data CO2 and Greenhouse Gas Emissions (owid-co2-data.csv); Global Carbon Budget. Retrieved 2026-08-05.",
 "_tier": "A \u2014 published dataset, covers 1750-2024",
 "_why": "CDR_ALLOCATION_BASIS = 'responsibility': a collective cannot burden others with the consequences of choices it made. Allocating the drawdown job by headcount silently transfers one collective's history onto everyone.",
 "_basis_choice": {
  "fossil": "cumulative_co2 \u2014 fossil fuels + cement. Lower uncertainty, but leaves ~33% of the atmospheric burden unallocated.",
  "incl_luc": "cumulative_co2_including_luc \u2014 adds land-use change. DEFAULT: it matches the physical target, since the drawdown must remove the whole atmospheric CO2 burden and the forcing coefficient was fitted to a concentration record that already reflects land-use emissions.",
  "OPEN \u2014 SIGN-OFF": "Including land use is the technically coherent basis but a live equity question: it moves substantial burden onto collectives that were often converting land under external demand, and LUC estimates carry much larger uncertainty than fossil. The framework cannot yet trade-adjust \u2014 OWID consumption-based emissions begin only in 1990, far too short for a cumulative measure."
 },
 "_truncation_warning": "A series truncated at a recent start year under-charges early industrializers, precisely the collectives the rule exists to charge. Measured against a 1965+ fossil-energy proxy: UK under-charged 1.70x, Germany 1.31x, Russia 1.16x, while Singapore was over-charged 4.1x and Qatar 1.70x. Do not use a truncated basis.",
 "_unattributed": {
  "note": "Collective shares sum to 0.975 (fossil) and 0.998 (incl. land use), NOT to 1. The residual is international shipping (29.6 GtCO2) and aviation (16.3 GtCO2) \u2014 46 GtCO2, 2.49% of the cumulative fossil total \u2014 which OWID reports separately because they belong to no territory.",
  "OPEN \u2014 SIGN-OFF": "Under a responsibility rule nobody owes the drawdown for bunker fuels. Three options: leave it unallocated (the commons absorbs it), redistribute pro-rata to responsibility shares (charges everyone for transport they may not have used), or allocate by consumption (needs trade data the framework does not have before 1990). Currently UNALLOCATED \u2014 shares are used as-is, so 2.5% of the obligation has no bearer.",
  "share_fossil_unattributed": 0.0249,
  "share_incl_luc_unattributed": 0.0019
 },
